8. THESIS-READY SUMMARY PARAGRAPH
====================================================================================================
  The A1 scaling experiment, replicated on the no-Flink (direct path) architecture, ramped
  from 1 to 20 concurrent producers, adding one device every 3 min for ~60 min total.
  Across all captured data (37,500 messages, 625 of 630 expected
  batches = 99% system coverage), median end-to-end latency was 30144 ms
  (p99 = 80622 ms, max = 154602 ms).
  At 1 active device the median latency was 29613 ms; at 20 active devices it was
  33139 ms (p99 = 133521 ms) -- a +3.5 s shift in p50 as concurrency grew.
  Coverage degraded gracefully: 100 % on devices 1-13, gradual fall to 33 % by device 20,
  versus the Flink path where multiple mid-stream devices (4, 8, 14, 16) lost >75 % of their
  expected output.  Schedule slippage was also smaller in the direct path (+218 s vs 565 s).
  These results place the practical saturation point of the no-Flink architecture, on this
  hardware, at roughly 19-20 concurrent producers - one to two devices beyond the
  Flink-path architecture's saturation point.  The bounded gain is consistent with the host-
  process memory model: removing jobmanager+taskmanager containers saves ~2 GiB of RAM, which
  accommodates roughly one extra device pipeline at this hardware tier.

